// A scene about the light a medium has already turned once.
//
// Everything up to here stopped at the first turn: light went from a lamp, was turned aside once, and
// arrived at the eye.  In anything thick that is a small share of what there is.  Nearly all the light
// leaving a real cloud has been turned a dozen times or more on the way out, bouncing from one part of
// it to another, and that is why a cloud is white rather than gray and why the side away from the sun
// glows instead of going black.
//
// This is the same ball of noise the shaped-density scene uses, made thicker and given almost nothing
// to absorb with, so that the light it stops is nearly all passed on rather than swallowed.  Such a
// medium is exactly the case single scattering gets most wrong: with `medium bounces 0` it comes out a
// dull gray lump with a dead black underside, because every one of those onward journeys is thrown
// away.  Set the bounces below to nothing and look, then set them back; it is the same cloud, and the
// difference is all light that was there to be found.
//
// What each turn is worth is the share of stopped light the medium passes on rather than swallowing --
// its albedo, which here is above ninety-five hundredths in every color.  That is why so many turns
// are worth following: at each one, almost nothing is lost, so the eighth turn still carries most of
// what the first did.  Give the same cloud a heavy absorption and three turns would be plenty, the
// rest having been swallowed long before.
//
// The cost is one more path for every turn, since each place followed picks a single direction to have
// come from rather than trying all of them, and many of those paths wander out of the cloud and end.
// Measured on this scene, six turns cost about two and a half times what none do.
//
// The grain in the shaded part is honest and worth understanding.  Where the cloud is lit directly the
// answer is well settled, but in the shade every scrap of light arrived by wandering, and each place
// along a ray sends one path off to find it.  Most of those paths find the lamp hidden and come back
// with nothing; now and again one reaches the lit crown and comes back with a great deal.  The average
// is right and settles as the square root of the sample count, which is to say slowly -- so a thicker,
// less absorbing cloud than this one grains up badly, and the cure is more places rather than more
// turns.  Note also that the grain is the same in every render rather than crawling, so it will not
// average away over the frames of an animation.
//
// What this scene leaves out, and what matters more than any of this for a daylight cloud: a sky.
// There is one lamp here and nothing else, so every scrap of light in the picture came from that one
// direction.  A `sky light` is gathered at every place along a ray and again at every turn of a
// followed path, and for a real cloud it is the larger part of the light there is -- `sunlit-cloud.igl`
// in this same directory is this idea with a sky over it, and the difference is not subtle.

context {
    parallel pixel scanner
    angles are degrees
    no gamma
    medium samples 144
    medium bounces 6
    info {
        title 'Multiple Scattering'
        author 'J. Stephen Kress'
        description 'A cloud lit by the light it has already turned, which is most of the light there is.'
        copyright true
        software __software__
    }
}

camera {
    location [0, 0.4, -6.5]
    look at [0, 0.1, 0]
    field of view 44
}

background [0.03, 0.045, 0.08]

// One lamp, above and a little behind, so that the underside of the cloud is lit by nothing directly.
// Whatever light reaches it got there through the cloud itself.
point light {
    location [-3.5, 5.5, 2.5]
    color [2.1, 2.05, 1.95]
}

sphere {
    material {
        pigment White
        ambient 0
        diffuse 0
        specular 0
        transparency 1
        interior {
            medium {
                // Thick enough that light must wander to get out, and swallowing almost none of what
                // it stops -- the two together are what make the turns worth following.  Drop the
                // scattering to a tenth of this and the first turn is nearly the whole story.
                scattering 2.4
                absorption 0.06
                anisotropy 0.35
                density function {
                    max(0, 1 - √(x² + y² + z²)) *
                    max(0, 2.1 * noise(2.7*x, 2.7*y, 2.7*z) - 0.5)
                }
            }
        }
    }
    scale 1.8
}
